== ERC-1967 Utils Library

The `Erc1967Utils` library provides helper functions for managing ERC-1967 storage slots:

=== Getting Values

[source,rust]
----
use openzeppelin_stylus::proxy::erc1967::utils::Erc1967Utils;
use stylus_sdk::alloy_primitives::Address;

impl MyErc1967Proxy {
    fn current_implementation(&self) -> Address {
        Erc1967Utils::get_implementation(self)
    }

    fn current_admin(&self) -> Address {
        Erc1967Utils::get_admin(self)
    }

    fn current_beacon(&self) -> Address {
        Erc1967Utils::get_beacon(self)
    }
}
----

=== Upgrading Implementation

[source,rust]
----
use openzeppelin_stylus::proxy::erc1967::utils::Erc1967Utils;
use stylus_sdk::{abi::Bytes, alloy_primitives::Address, prelude::*};

impl MyErc1967Proxy {
    /// Upgrade to a new implementation
    fn upgrade_implementation(
        &mut self,
        new_implementation: Address,
        data: Bytes,
    ) -> Result<(), erc1967::utils::Error> {
        Erc1967Utils::upgrade_to_and_call(self, new_implementation, &data)
    }
}
----

=== Changing Admin

[source,rust]
----
use openzeppelin_stylus::proxy::erc1967::utils::Erc1967Utils;

impl MyErc1967Proxy {
    /// Change the admin address
    fn change_admin(&mut self, new_admin: Address) -> Result<(), erc1967::utils::Error> {
        Erc1967Utils::change_admin(self, new_admin)
    }
}
----

=== Upgrading Beacon

[source,rust]
----
use openzeppelin_stylus::proxy::erc1967::utils::Erc1967Utils;
use stylus_sdk::{abi::Bytes, prelude::*};

impl MyErc1967Proxy {
    /// Upgrade to a new beacon
    fn upgrade_beacon(
        &mut self,
        new_beacon: Address,
        data: Bytes,
    ) -> Result<(), erc1967::utils::Error> {
        Erc1967Utils::upgrade_beacon_to_and_call(self, new_beacon, &data)
    }
}
----

== Constructor Data

The ERC-1967 proxy constructor accepts optional initialization data:
= ERC-20 Permit
Implementation of the ERC20 Permit extension allowing approvals to be made via signatures, as defined in https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-2612[`EIP-2612`].

Adds the permit method, which can be used to change an account’s ERC20 allowance (see https://docs.rs/openzeppelin-stylus/0.4.0/openzeppelin_stylus/token/erc20/trait.IErc20.html#tymethod.allowance[`IErc20::allowance`]) by presenting a message signed by the account. By not relying on https://docs.rs/openzeppelin-stylus/0.4.0/openzeppelin_stylus/token/erc20/trait.IErc20.html#tymethod.approve[`IErc20::approve`], the token holder account doesn’t need to send a transaction, and thus is not required to hold Ether at all.


[[usage]]
== Usage

In order to have https://docs.rs/openzeppelin-stylus/0.4.0/openzeppelin_stylus/token/erc20/extensions/permit/index.html[`ERC-20 Permit`] token, you need to use only this contract without xref:erc20.adoc[ERC-20] as follows:

[source,rust]
----
extern crate alloc;
use alloc::vec::Vec;
use openzeppelin_stylus::{
    token::erc20::{
        extensions::{permit, Erc20Permit, IErc20Permit},
        Erc20, IErc20,
    },
    utils::{
        cryptography::eip712::IEip712,
        nonces::{INonces, Nonces},
    },
};
use stylus_sdk::{alloy_primitives::{Address, B256, U256}, prelude::*};

#[entrypoint]
#[storage]
struct Erc20PermitExample {
    erc20: Erc20,
    nonces: Nonces,
    erc20_permit: Erc20Permit<Eip712>,
}

#[storage]
struct Eip712;

impl IEip712 for Eip712 {
    const NAME: &'static str = "ERC-20 Permit Example";
    const VERSION: &'static str = "1";

    fn chain_id(&self) -> U256 {
        U256::from(self.vm().chain_id())
    }

    fn contract_address(&self) -> Address {
        self.vm().contract_address()
    }
}

#[public]
#[implements(IErc20<Error = permit::Error>, INonces, IErc20Permit<Error = permit::Error>)]
impl Erc20PermitExample {
    // Add token minting feature.
    fn mint(
        &mut self,
        account: Address,
        value: U256,
    ) -> Result<(), permit::Error> {
        Ok(self.erc20._mint(account, value)?)
    }
}

#[public]
impl INonces for Erc20PermitExample {
    fn nonces(&self, owner: Address) -> U256 {
        self.nonces.nonces(owner)
    }
}

#[public]
impl IErc20Permit for Erc20PermitExample {
    type Error = permit::Error;

        token_ids: Vec<U256>,
    ) -> Result<bool, Self::Error> {
        self.erc721_wrapper.deposit_for(account, token_ids, &mut self.erc721)
    }

    fn withdraw_to(
        &mut self,
        account: Address,
        token_ids: Vec<U256>,
    ) -> Result<bool, Self::Error> {
        self.erc721_wrapper.withdraw_to(account, token_ids, &mut self.erc721)
    }
}

#[public]
impl IErc721Receiver for Erc721WrapperExample {
    #[selector(name = "onERC721Received")]
    fn on_erc721_received(
        &mut self,
        operator: Address,
        from: Address,
        token_id: U256,
        data: Bytes,
    ) -> Result<B32, Vec<u8>> {
        self.erc721_wrapper
            .on_erc721_received(
                operator,
                from,
                token_id,
                &data,
                &mut self.erc721,
            )
            .map_err(|e| e.into())
    }
}

#[public]
impl IErc165 for Erc721WrapperExample {
    fn supports_interface(&self, interface_id: B32) -> bool {
        self.erc721.supports_interface(interface_id)
    }
== Public API and behavior notes

* ERC-20 cap checks can be shared through `Capped::check_increase(total_supply, amount)`. It returns the standard `ExceededCap` error payload and does not change storage.
* When composing `Capped` with `Erc20FlashMint`, implement the lender methods with `max_flash_loan_with_cap` and `flash_loan_with_cap`. The general flash-mint helpers do not discover sibling extensions and remain uncapped; using them with a capped token can exceed the cap during a loan callback.
* `Erc721Wrapper` now rejects deposits, withdrawals, and receiver callbacks when its configured underlying address has no code. Previously, an EOA could make a no-return call appear successful, allowing an unbacked wrapper mint or a burn with no underlying transfer, or could call the receiver callback directly to mint an unbacked token. This is an intentional security fix; configure a deployed ERC-721 contract before using the wrapper.
* `Erc20Wrapper::_recover` now reports the configured underlying token in `ERC20InvalidUnderlying` when the underlying `balanceOf` call fails. The previous error encoded the wrapper's own address.
* Contracts implementing `IErc721Receiver` must expose the canonical `onERC721Received` selector. The receiver trait now specifies it explicitly so generated ABI and interface ID metadata agree with ERC-721.
* Poseidon2 construction now rejects round exponents that are not invertible modulo `p - 1`. Such exponents do not define permutations and can produce collisions. Review any custom Poseidon2 parameter sets and select a valid exponent.
* `BeaconProxy` keeps its original storage field as a reserved slot, but reads the active beacon from the ERC-1967 beacon slot. v0.3 proxies already wrote that slot. During v0.3 proxy construction the proxy also wrote its beacon address to ordinary slot zero after delegate initialization; if the implementation used slot zero, that field may have been overwritten. The migration cannot recover that value. Keep the existing implementation field order, treat slot zero as reserved for existing deployments, and reconstruct any overwritten application value from trusted state before relying on it.
* `uups_upgradeable::initializer(storage)` adds a one-time marker in a namespaced slot without changing the contract's storage layout or public ABI. Call it from each initializer before assigning privileged state. Existing v0.3 proxies have no marker: the new implementation must also recognize its existing initialized state before allowing initialization. The UUPS examples use their preserved version field as well as the owner and now restrict `setVersion` to the owner, so an old proxy remains marked even after ownership is renounced. Apply the same legacy-state check for your contract's authorization model before accepting initialization after an upgrade.
* `Erc721Enumerable` now recognizes token ID zero in owner enumeration by tracking its presence in an unstructured slot. This leaves the storage struct layout unchanged. Previously deployed ID-zero entries have no presence marker; build and review a migration for those tokens before relying on owner enumeration after upgrading.
* The EdDSA example now rejects affine coordinates outside the Curve25519 field and scalars at or above the scalar modulus. Callers that sent reduced, noncanonical aliases must encode canonical values.
* `Erc721Consecutive::_mint_consecutive` remains an internal helper that does not check whether it runs during construction. Callers must keep it constructor-only because it omits per-token `Transfer` events and receiver callbacks. The ERC-721 Consecutive docs now show the SDK 0.10 `#[implements]` composition style.
* The token and proxy examples are composition demonstrations. Several intentionally expose unrestricted mint or fee-setting calls; put access control around privileged operations before deploying a production contract.
* Contract storage fields, proxy slots, and event signatures remain unchanged by the SDK migration. The ERC-721 receiver now exposes the canonical `onERC721Received` selector; v0.3 emitted the noncanonical camel-case spelling. Update integrations that called the old selector. Other behavior changes above affect invalid configurations or invalid parameter sets only.

== Adding stateful extensions to deployed proxies

Keeping existing fields in place preserves their slots, but it does not initialize a newly added extension's auxiliary state. Do not add these extensions to a live proxy without an explicit backfill plan:

